WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.846 --> 00:00:03.621
A bakery starts a project against downtime.

2
00:00:04.317 --> 00:00:08.543
At the kick-off you, the consultant, sit with six people in the meeting room.

3
00:00:09.246 --> 00:00:11.069
You take 20 minutes for this.

4
00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:13.285
You run a pre-mortem.

5
00:00:13.986 --> 00:00:18.467
You say: imagine it is six months from now, and this project has failed.

6
00:00:19.527 --> 00:00:21.768
Write down, alone, why it failed.

7
00:00:22.530 --> 00:00:24.817
Everyone writes on their own first.

8
00:00:25.636 --> 00:00:30.570
Then you collect one reason per person per round, until everything is on the board.

9
00:00:31.637 --> 00:00:34.365
Writing alone first gets you everyone's view,

10
00:00:34.482 --> 00:00:37.187
so the loudest voice does not set the direction.

11
00:00:37.880 --> 00:00:41.421
Gary Klein described the method in 2007.

12
00:00:42.733 --> 00:00:43.511
What comes up?

13
00:00:44.198 --> 00:00:48.390
Three people write: maintenance never had time, because of the summer peak.

14
00:00:49.073 --> 00:00:53.136
Someone else: the operators stopped logging stops after two weeks.

15
00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:55.934
And: the new manager cancelled it.

16
00:00:56.969 --> 00:00:59.419
Because the failure is imagined as a fact,

17
00:00:59.617 --> 00:01:02.427
people name things they would not say otherwise.

18
00:01:03.117 --> 00:01:04.441
The sponsor might leave.

19
00:01:05.151 --> 00:01:06.893
The night shift won't accept it.

20
00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:09.438
The IT change has no budget.

21
00:01:10.471 --> 00:01:14.186
Klein cites research from 1989 here.

22
00:01:14.892 --> 00:01:15.728
By his account,

23
00:01:15.914 --> 00:01:18.688
looking back from an imagined future makes people

24
00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:21.614
30 percent better at naming the right reasons.

25
00:01:22.941 --> 00:01:25.600
Now you turn the top reasons into risks.

26
00:01:26.369 --> 00:01:29.689
A risk is something that might happen and would hurt the project.

27
00:01:30.744 --> 00:01:32.335
A risk register lists them.

28
00:01:33.038 --> 00:01:37.102
Each risk gets a likelihood, an impact, meaning how bad it is,

29
00:01:37.287 --> 00:01:39.330
an owner, and an action with a date.

30
00:01:40.375 --> 00:01:43.904
The risk owner is one person who watches it and acts.

31
00:01:44.639 --> 00:01:45.730
Not a department.

32
00:01:46.798 --> 00:01:49.642
A line like: data may be poor, helps no one.

33
00:01:50.399 --> 00:01:51.189
This one does:

34
00:01:51.328 --> 00:01:55.334
the maintenance data from before 2023 may be

35
00:01:55.426 --> 00:01:57.911
unusable after the move to the new system.

36
00:01:58.644 --> 00:01:59.596
Owner: Pieter.

37
00:02:00.343 --> 00:02:02.793
Action: a test export in week 1.

38
00:02:04.155 --> 00:02:08.822
Many teams score likelihood and impact from 1 to 5, and multiply them.

39
00:02:09.542 --> 00:02:12.131
A risk then scores 1 to 25.

40
00:02:13.164 --> 00:02:16.334
Treat the score as a way to sort, not as the truth.

41
00:02:17.048 --> 00:02:19.335
A 12 is not really worse than a 10.

42
00:02:20.321 --> 00:02:21.923
The action is the mitigation:

43
00:02:22.039 --> 00:02:25.267
what you do now to make the risk less likely or less harmful.

44
00:02:25.973 --> 00:02:27.320
Monitoring is not one.

45
00:02:28.036 --> 00:02:30.091
A test export in week 1 is.

46
00:02:31.112 --> 00:02:32.227
Back to the bakery.

47
00:02:32.929 --> 00:02:35.971
The summer peak: likelihood high, impact high.

48
00:02:36.672 --> 00:02:38.797
The owner is the maintenance manager.

49
00:02:39.519 --> 00:02:44.558
Action: plan the analysis before June, and reserve 4 hours a week per technician.

50
00:02:45.551 --> 00:02:47.978
The logging: the owner is the shift lead.

51
00:02:48.710 --> 00:02:54.051
Action: one-tap logging on the line terminal, the screen at the line, by week 3.

52
00:02:55.332 --> 00:02:58.153
Most risk registers die after the kick-off.

53
00:02:58.858 --> 00:03:03.247
Keep yours alive: only the top five are discussed in each steering group.

54
00:03:04.023 --> 00:03:07.796
At the bakery, every steering group starts with these five.

55
00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:12.303
And for each risk, the owner says what has happened since.

56
00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:15.552
Add new risks as soon as you hear them.

57
00:03:16.259 --> 00:03:18.558
You often hear them at the coffee machine.

58
00:03:19.601 --> 00:03:23.931
When a risk comes true, it becomes an issue: a problem happening now.

59
00:03:24.649 --> 00:03:26.843
You handle it; you don't just watch it.

60
00:03:28.242 --> 00:03:29.426
Then the people side.

61
00:03:30.155 --> 00:03:35.171
Some risks are sensitive, like: the plant manager doesn't really believe in this.

62
00:03:35.869 --> 00:03:39.178
Don't write that in a register the plant manager reads.

63
00:03:40.251 --> 00:03:43.676
Word it as a theme: sponsorship and visible support.

64
00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:48.225
And discuss the real point one-on-one, with whoever can act on it.

65
00:03:49.257 --> 00:03:51.045
Don't know how likely something is?

66
00:03:51.764 --> 00:03:54.678
Say so, and plan a cheap test to find out.

67
00:03:55.782 --> 00:03:58.719
And ask one sharp question for each top risk:

68
00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:01.552
what would we see first if this starts to happen?
